editorial_cleaned:
Let $\mathcal{dp}(l, r, root)$ be a dp determining whether it's possible to assemble a tree rooted at $root$ from the subsegment $[l..r]$.
It's easy to see that calculating it requires extracting such $root_{left}$ from $[l..root - 1]$ and $root_{right}$ from $[root + 1..right]$ that:
• $\mathcal{gcd}(a_{root}, a_{root_{left}}) > 1$;
• $\mathcal{gcd}(a_{root}, a_{root_{right}}) > 1$;
• $\mathcal{dp}(l, root - 1, root_{left}) = 1$;
• $\mathcal{dp}(root + 1, r, root_{right}) = 1$.
This can be done in $\mathcal{O}(r - l)$ provided we are given all $\mathcal{dp}(x, y, z)$ values for all subsegments of $[l..r]$. Considering a total of $\mathcal{O}(n^3)$ dp states, the final complexity is $\mathcal{O}(n^4)$. That's too much.
Let's turn our dp into $\mathcal{dp_{new}}(l, r, side)$ where $side$ can be either $0$ or $1$ — is it possible to make a tree from $[l..r]$ rooted at $l - 1$ or $r + 1$ respectively. It immediately turns out that $\mathcal{dp}(l, r, root)$ is inherited form $\mathcal{dp_{new}}(l, root - 1, 1)$ and $\mathcal{dp_{new}}(root + 1, r, 0)$. Now we have $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ states, but at the same time all transitions are performed in linear time; thus final complexity is $\mathcal{O}(n^3)$ which is sufficient to pass.

