Now, the key idea is to consider a binary $\overline{s} = (s_1\oplus s_2)(s_2\oplus s_3) \dots (s_{n-1}\oplus s_n)$ of length $n-1$, where $a\oplus b$ denotes the XOR operation of bits $a$ and $b$. Then, it's easy to verify that the operation acts on $\overline{s}$ by just swapping two different bits. An example is shown below

 $ \begin{array}{cc} s & \overline s \\ \texttt{000101} & \texttt{00111} \\ \downarrow & \downarrow \\ \texttt{001101} & \texttt{01011}\\ \downarrow & \downarrow \\ \texttt{011101} & \texttt{10011} \\ \downarrow & \downarrow \\ \texttt{011001} & \texttt{10101} \\ \downarrow & \downarrow \\ \texttt{011011} & \texttt{10110} \\ \downarrow & \downarrow \\ \texttt{010011} & \texttt{11010} \end{array}$

