F. Kosuke's Sloth

The Fibonacci numbers divisible by $k$ appear periodically. Find the period (Pisano-like): simulate the Fibonacci sequence modulo $k$ and detect when we see $f \equiv 0, f_{\text{next}} \equiv 1$ (indicating the sequence has cycled back). Count how many multiples of $k$ appear in one period; call this count $\text{cnt}$, and the period length $P$. Then $G(n, k) = P \cdot n / \text{cnt}$, i.e., $G(n, k) = n \cdot P / \text{cnt}$. Output this modulo $10^9 + 7$ (using modular inverse for division).

For $k = 1$, every Fibonacci number is divisible by $1$, so $G(n, 1) = n$.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;
using LL = long long;
#define ssize(x) (int)(x.size)
#define ALL(x) (x).begin, (x).end

mt19937 rng(chrono::steady_clock::now.time_since_epoch.count);

int rd(int l, int r) {
    return uniform_int_distribution<int>(l, r)(rng);
}
const LL MOD = 1e9 + 7;
int bp(int a, int n) {
    if (n == 0)
        return 1;
    if (n % 2 == 0)
        return bp(1LL * a * a % MOD, n / 2);
    else
        return 1LL * bp(a, n - 1) * a % MOD;
