G. Sakurako and Chefir

Root the tree at vertex $1$. For each vertex $v$, precompute:
- $h[v]$: depth of $v$ (distance from root)
- $\text{maxd}[v]$: the two longest downward paths from $v$ (stored as absolute depths, i.e., $h[v] + \text{length}$)

For a query $(v, k)$: Chefir can go up at most $k$ steps (each upward move costs $1$ stamina, but $k$ is capped at $h[v]$ since we can't go above the root). At each ancestor we visit, we can go down for free into that ancestor's subtree. So we want to find, over all ancestors $u$ reachable within $k$ upward moves, the maximum over $\text{maxd}[u].x$ (excluding the direction we came from). The answer is $\max(\text{maxd}[u].x) - h[v] + h[v] = \max(\text{maxd}[u].x) - h[v] + \text{initial depth}$, i.e., the farthest reachable vertex depth minus the starting depth gives distance.

Use binary lifting: $\text{binup}[v][i]$ stores the best answer reachable by going $2^i$ steps up from $v$, and the ancestor reached. When going from child $v$ to parent $p$, the best downward distance from $p$ not going through $v$ is $\text{maxd}[p].x$ if $\text{maxd}[p].x \neq \text{maxd}[v].x + 1$, else $\text{maxd}[p].y$. Store these as absolute depths (subtract $h[p]$ to normalize).

For each query, binary-lift upward at most $\min(k, h[v])$ steps, taking the maximum stored value, then return $\text{best\_absolute\_depth} - h[v] + h[v] = \text{best\_absolute\_depth} - h[v_{\text{original}}] + h[v_{\text{original}}]$... more precisely: result $= \text{res} + \text{ini}$ where $\text{ini} = h[v]$ at query start and $\text{res}$ is the maximum of (stored value $+ h[v_{\text{original}}] - h[v_{\text{original}}]$) tracked during lifting.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

