This is a DP problem. Let $dp[i][j]$ be the minimum damage when we have performed $i$ physical upgrades and $j$ elemental upgrades (so physical attack power is $i+1$ and elemental is $j+1$). We process events (monster appearances and kill opportunities) in time order.

At each step (there are $2n$ steps total), we either have a new monster appear (first $n$ steps) or just have a kill opportunity. At each step, with current upgrades $(i,j)$, the number of killable monsters so far is $\text{cnt}[i][j]$ (monsters with $a_t \le i$ or $b_t \le j$). The number of monsters killed so far equals $i+j$ upgrades received. We must kill one monster to get an upgrade, so we need $\text{cnt}[i][j] > i+j$ to be able to upgrade. If we can upgrade, we transition to $(i+1,j)$ or $(i,j+1)$ with cost = number of currently alive monsters minus the one we're killing. Otherwise we must wait.

**Solution (Neon)**
```kotlin
const val N = 505
const val INF = 1e9.toInt

fun main {
  val n = readln.toInt
  val a = readln.split(" ").map { it.toInt - 1}
  val b = readln.split(" ").map { it.toInt - 1}

  val cnt = Array(N) { IntArray(N) { 0 } }
  val dp = Array(2) { Array(N) { IntArray(N) { INF } } }
  dp[0][0][0] = 0

  for (t in 0 until 2 * n) {
    val ct = t and 1
    val nt = ct xor 1

    if (t < n) {
      for (i in 0 until N) for (j in 0 until N) {
        if (a[t] <= i || b[t] <= j) ++cnt[i][j];
      }
    }

    dp[nt].forEach { it.fill(INF) }
    for (i in 0 until N) for (j in 0 until N) {
      if (dp[ct][i][j] != INF) {
        val lft = minOf(t + 1, n) - i - j
        if (cnt[i][j] > i + j) {
          dp[nt][i + 1][j] = minOf(dp[nt][i + 1][j], dp[ct][i][j] + lft - 1)
          dp[nt][i][j + 1] = minOf(dp[nt][i][j + 1], dp[ct][i][j] + lft - 1)
        } else {
          if (t < n || lft == 0) dp[nt][i][j] = minOf(dp[nt][i][j], dp[ct][i][j] + lft)
        }
      }
    }
  }

  var ans = INF
  for (i in 0 until N) for (j in 0 until N) ans = minOf(ans, dp[0][i][j])
  if (ans == INF) ans = -1
  println(ans)
}
```

Suppose Monocarp trains for $k$ days (out of $n$). The total income if he skips $k$ specific days is $\sum_{i \notin \text{skipped}} (a_i + s_i)$ where $s_i$ is his skill level on day $i$. If he trains on the $k$ days with the largest "benefit", his skill grows over time. 

If he trains exactly $k$ days total, his skill level when participating on day $i$ depends on how many training days are before day $i$. Optimally, the training days should be the first $k$ days. But which $k$ days should be training days?

Let $\delta_i = (n - i) - a_i$ (0-indexed). If we sort $\delta$ in descending order and take the top $k$ values, the total income for training exactly $k$ days is:
$$\text{income}(k) = \sum a_i + \sum_{\text{top }k} \delta_i - \binom{k}{2}$$

We iterate over all $k$ from $0$ to $n$, compute the value, track the maximum and count the number of ways to achieve it using combinatorics (choosing which $k$ elements from the tied group).

**Solution (adedalic)**
```kotlin
fun main {
    data class ModInt(val value : Int = 0) {
        val MOD = 998244353
        operator fun plus(oth : ModInt) : ModInt {
            val cur = (this.value + oth.value) % MOD
            return ModInt(cur)
        }
        operator fun times(oth : ModInt) : ModInt {
            val cur = value.toLong * oth.value % MOD
            return ModInt(cur.toInt)
        }
        fun pow(k: Int) : ModInt {
            var ans = ModInt(1)
            var a = ModInt(value)
            var curPw = k
            while (curPw > 0) {
                if (curPw % 2 == 1)
                    ans *= a
                a *= a
                curPw /= 2
            }
            return ans
        }
        fun inverse = this.pow(MOD - 2)
        override fun toString = this.value.toString
    }

    val n = readln.toInt
    val a = readln.split(" ").map { it.toInt }

    val factorials = (1..n).runningFold(ModInt(1)) { acc, it -> acc * ModInt(it) }
    val invFact = factorials.map { it.inverse }

    fun choose(n : Int, k : Int) : ModInt {
        if (k < 0 || n < k)
            return ModInt(0)
        return factorials[n] * invFact[k] * invFact[n - k]
    }

    val sum = a.sumOf { it.toLong }
    val delta = List(n) { n - it - 1 - a[it] }.sortedDescending

    var ansVal = sum
    var ansCnt = ModInt(1)

    var curSum = 0L
    var lf = 0
    var rg = 0
    for (k in 1..n) {
        curSum += delta[k - 1]
        while (delta[lf] > delta[k - 1])
            lf++
        while (rg < delta.size && delta[rg] >= delta[k - 1])
            rg++
        val curVal = sum + curSum - k.toLong * (k - 1) / 2
        val curCnt = choose(rg - lf, k - lf)
        if (ansVal < curVal) {
            ansVal = curVal
            ansCnt = ModInt(0)
        }
        if (ansVal == curVal) {
            ansCnt += curCnt
        }
    }
    println("$ansVal $ansCnt")
}
```

description:
Let's recall the rules of the game "Nim". The game is played by two players who take turns. They have a certain number of piles of stones (the piles can be of the same size or different sizes; the size is defined as the number of stones in a pile). On their turn, a player must choose any non-empty pile of stones and remove any number of stones from it (at least one). The player who cannot make a move loses.

In this problem, a modified version of the game will be used. There is a special set of numbers $$$S$$$ that prohibits certain moves. For each number $$$s_i$$$ in this set, if the current size of the pile of stones is strictly greater than $$$s_i$$$, it cannot be made strictly less than $$$s_i$$$ in one move. In other words, if the current size of the pile is $$$x$$$, and the player tries to make a move that results in a size of $$$y$$$, and $$$x  \gt  s_i  \gt  y$$$ for some number $$$s_i \in S$$$, such a move is not allowed.

Your task is as follows. Initially, the set $$$S$$$ is empty. You need to process queries of three types:

•  add a certain number $$$s_i$$$ to the set;
•  remove a certain number $$$s_i$$$ from the set;
•  determine who will win if this modified version of Nim is played with piles of stones of sizes $$$a_1, a_2, \dots, a_k$$$. Assume that both players play optimally.

editorial_cleaned:
In standard Nim, the Grundy value of a pile of size $x$ is $x$. With a forbidden set $S$, moving from pile size $x$ cannot skip over any $s \in S$. So the reachable positions from $x$ are $\{y : y < x, \nexists s \in S: y < s < x\}$, i.e., all values in the interval $(\text{prev}(x), x]$ where $\text{prev}(x)$ is the largest element of $S$ less than $x$ (treating 0 as always in $S$).

The Grundy value of $x$ is the mex of Grundy values of all reachable positions. Since the reachable positions from $x$ are all values in $[\text{prev}(x), x)$ union $\{x-1, x-2, \ldots\}$ within the interval, the Grundy values within a segment $(s_{i-1}, s_i]$ form an arithmetic-like pattern.

We maintain a segment tree where each node stores a function (as a node with a "split point" and two output values) mapping the input Grundy value of the right endpoint to the Grundy value of $x$. Specifically, each element of $S$ contributes a "boundary" node. The segment tree combines these functions via composition. To answer a query for pile size $x$, we query the prefix $[0, x]$ of the segment tree to get the composed function, then evaluate it at 0.

Updates to $S$ (add/remove an element) require updating at most 2 positions in the segment tree (the element itself and possibly its successor). Each update is $O(\log N)$. Each query is $O(\log N)$, and with $k \le 3$ piles, each type-2 query is $O(k \log N)$.

**Solution (BledDest)**
```kotlin
import java.util.*

data class Node(var x: Int = 0, var a: Int = 0, var b: Int = 0)

fun get(n: Node, x: Int): Int {
    return when {
        n.x == -1 -> x
        x < n.x -> n.a
        else -> n.b
    }
}

fun combine(a: Node, b: Node): Node {
    return when {
        a.x == -1 -> b
        b.x == -1 -> a
        else -> Node(a.x, get(b, a.a), get(b, a.b))
    }
}

fun emptyNode: Node {
    return Node(-1, -1, -1)
}

fun fromLength(len: Int): Node {
    return if (len == -1) emptyNode else Node(len, len, len - 1)
}

const val N = 300003
const val M = 300001

val S = sortedSetOf<Int>
val T = Array<Node>(4 * N) { emptyNode }

fun recalc(v: Int) {
    T[v] = combine(T[v * 2 + 1], T[v * 2 + 2])
}

fun build(v: Int, l: Int, r: Int) {
    if (l == r - 1) {
        T[v] = emptyNode
    } else {
        val m = (l + r) / 2
        build(v * 2 + 1, l, m)
        build(v * 2 + 2, m, r)
        recalc(v)
    }
}

fun upd(v: Int, l: Int, r: Int, pos: Int, x: Int) {
    if (l == r - 1) {
        T[v] = fromLength(x)
    } else {
        val m = (l + r) / 2
        if (pos < m) upd(v * 2 + 1, l, m, pos, x)
        else upd(v * 2 + 2, m, r, pos, x)
        recalc(v)
    }
}

fun get(v: Int, l: Int, r: Int, L: Int, R: Int): Node {
    if (L >= R) return emptyNode
    if (L == l && R == r) return T[v]
    val m = (l + r) / 2
    return combine(get(v * 2 + 1, l, m, L, minOf(m, R)), get(v * 2 + 2, m, r, maxOf(L, m), R))
}

fun prepare {
    build(0, 0, M)
    S.add(0)
}

fun insertValue(x: Int) {
    val it = S.ceiling(x)
    if (it != null) {
    }
    S.lower(x)?.let { upd(0, 0, M, x, x - it) }
    S.add(x)
}

fun eraseValue(x: Int) {
    S.remove(x)
    val it = S.ceiling(x)
    if (it != null) {
        val y = it
        S.lower(y)?.let { upd(0, 0, M, y, y - it) }
    }
}

fun getGrundy(x: Int): Int {
    val n = get(0, 0, M, 0, x)
    val it = S.lower(x)
    val y = it ?: return x
    val valY = get(n, 0)
    val dist = x - y
    return if (dist <= valY) dist - 1 else dist
}

fun main {
    val q = readln.toInt

    prepare
    println((0 until q).map {
        val params = readln.split(" ").map { it.toInt }
