We need to partition all edges of the DAG into Hamiltonian cycles, adding the minimum number of edges. 

First, the number of cycles $c$ must be at least $\max_v(\text{indeg}(v), \text{outdeg}(v))$, since in each Hamiltonian cycle every vertex has exactly one incoming and one outgoing edge.

With $c$ cycles, we need each vertex to have exactly $c$ outgoing and $c$ incoming edges total (original + added). So we need to assign each existing edge to one of $c$ colors (cycles) such that each color class uses each vertex at most once as a source and at most once as a destination. This is an edge coloring of a bipartite graph (left copies = sources, right copies = destinations) with $c$ colors.

We use a greedy edge coloring algorithm: for each edge $(u, v)$, find a color available at both $u$ (as source) and $v$ (as destination). If the same color is available at both, use it. Otherwise, find colors $c_u$ (free at $u$) and $c_v$ (free at $v$), and augment along the path in the color graph to make $c_u$ available at $v$ as well.

After coloring, each color class is a set of paths/chains (since it's a DAG). Connect the chains in each color into a single Hamiltonian cycle by adding edges from the end of each chain to the start of the next chain (in circular order). The number of added edges per color equals the number of connected components (chains) in that color.

