Count the number of zeros (`cnt[0]`) and ones (`cnt[1]`) in the string. There are $n/2$ pairs of indices $(i, n-i+1)$. A good pair requires both characters to be equal (both 0 or both 1).

The minimum number of good pairs is $\max(cnt[0], cnt[1]) - n/2$ (when the majority character is forced to pair with itself), denoted `mn`. The maximum number of good pairs is $\lfloor cnt[0]/2 \rfloor + \lfloor cnt[1]/2 \rfloor$, denoted `mx`.

Any value of $k$ between `mn` and `mx` that has the same parity as `mn` is achievable (since each swap of characters changes the number of good pairs by $\pm 2$).

