We want to minimize the maximum value after performing exactly one operation (choosing row $r$ and column $c$, then decrementing all cells in row $r$ or column $c$ by 1).

The answer is either $\text{mx}$ or $\text{mx} - 1$, where $\text{mx}$ is the current maximum. The answer is $\text{mx} - 1$ if and only if there exists a choice of $(r, c)$ such that every cell with value $\text{mx}$ is in row $r$ or column $c$.

To check this: let $\text{cnt\_mx}$ be the number of cells with value $\text{mx}$. For each row $i$, let $r[i]$ be the number of maximum-valued cells in row $i$. For each column $j$, let $c[j]$ be the number of maximum-valued cells in column $j$. Then for a pair $(i, j)$, the number of maximum-valued cells covered is $r[i] + c[j] - [a[i][j] == \text{mx}]$. If this equals $\text{cnt\_mx}$ for some $(i, j)$, then we can reduce the maximum by 1.

