We want to count subsegments $[l, r]$ such that the sum equals $s$ and the maximum equals $x$.

The idea is: a subsegment has maximum exactly $x$ if it contains at least one element equal to $x$ and no element greater than $x$.

We use a sliding window / two-pointer approach. Maintain a pointer `lef` representing the leftmost valid start. For each right endpoint $r$:
- If $a[r] > x$: no valid subsegment can end at $r$ or start before $r+1$, so clear the map and set `lef = r + 1`.
- If $a[r] = x$: all positions from `lef` to $r$ become valid starting points (they are guaranteed to include an element equal to $x$ when ending at $r$), so add all prefix sums $\text{pref}[\text{lef}-1], \ldots, \text{pref}[r-1]$ to the map, then advance `lef` to $r+1$.
- If $a[r] < x$: we don't add new starts (they haven't yet seen an $x$). We query the map for $\text{pref}[r] - s$.

When $a[r] = x$, we also query the map after inserting.

