**Hint 1:** Does it make sense to use the same type of deal more than 2 times?

**Hint 2:** The solution with the minimum number of deals is unique.

**Hint 3:** Ternary numeral system.

Observe that the deal for $3^x$ watermelons costs $3^{x+1} + x \cdot 3^{x-1}$ coins. Notice that 3 deals of type $x$ cost $3 \cdot (3^{x+1} + x \cdot 3^{x-1}) = 3^{x+2} + x \cdot 3^x$ coins and give $3^{x+1}$ watermelons, while 1 deal of type $x+1$ costs $3^{x+2} + (x+1) \cdot 3^x$ coins for the same number of watermelons. Since $(x+1) \cdot 3^x > x \cdot 3^x$, it is always cheaper to use 3 deals of type $x$ than 1 deal of type $x+1$. Thus the minimum number of deals corresponds to representing $n$ in base 3 and using exactly $d_i$ deals of type $i$ (where $d_i$ is the $i$-th ternary digit). This is the unique minimum-deal solution.

Precompute the cost of each deal type, then read $n$, extract its base-3 digits, and sum up $d_i \cdot \text{cost}[i]$.

```
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

int main {
    vector <long long> cost;
    long long c = 3;
    long long cnt = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < 21; ++i) {
        cost.push_back(c);
        c = 3 * c + cnt;
        cnt *= 3;
    }

    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while (t--) {
        long long n;
        cin >> n;
        long long min_k = 0;
        long long min_cost = 0;
        int sz = 0;
        while (n) {
            min_k += n % 3;
            min_cost += (n % 3) * cost[sz];
            n /= 3;
            sz++;
        }
        cout << min_cost << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Hint 1:** What is more profitable: 3 deals for $3^x$ watermelons each, or 1 deal for $3^{x+1}$ watermelons?

**Hint 2:** Recall the solution to problem C1.

From C1, we know the deal for $3^x$ watermelons costs $3^{x+1} + x \cdot 3^{x-1}$ coins, and the minimum number of deals representation is the base-3 representation of $n$. Let $\text{min\_k}$ be the sum of base-3 digits of $n$.

If $\text{min\_k} > k$, output $-1$ (impossible).

Otherwise, we have $k - \text{min\_k}$ extra deals to "spend". Each time we convert one deal of type $i+1$ into three deals of type $i$ (splitting a larger deal into smaller ones), the number of deals increases by 2 but the cost decreases (since 3 deals of type $x$ are cheaper than 1 deal of type $x+1$, as shown in C1). So we greedily split from the highest level downward, using up to $\lfloor (k - \text{min\_k}) / 2 \rfloor$ such splits to minimize cost.

Specifically, after computing the base-3 representation stored in array `tr`, we have $\lfloor (k - \text{min\_k}) / 2 \rfloor$ splits available. We iterate from the highest digit downward: for each digit $tr[i]$, we can split all $tr[i]$ deals of level $i$ into $3 \cdot tr[i]$ deals of level $i-1$ (increasing the number of deals by $2 \cdot tr[i]$, costing $tr[i]$ splits). If we have enough splits, do all of them; otherwise split as many as we can afford.

```
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

int main {
    vector <long long> cost;
    long long c = 3;
    long long cnt = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < 21; ++i) {
        cost.push_back(c);
        c = 3 * c + cnt;
        cnt *= 3;
    }

    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while (t--) {
        long long n, k;
        cin >> n >> k;
        vector <long long> tr;
        long long min_k = 0;
        while (n) {
            tr.push_back(n % 3);
            min_k += n % 3;
            n /= 3;
        }
        if (min_k > k) {
            cout << -1 << '\n';
            continue;
        }
        k -= min_k;
        k /= 2;
        for (int i = (int)tr.size - 1; i >= 1; --i) {
            if (tr[i] <= k) {
                tr[i - 1] += 3 * tr[i];
                k -= tr[i];
                tr[i] = 0;
            } else {
                tr[i - 1] += k * 3;
                tr[i] -= k;
                break;
            }
        }
        long long an = 0;
        for (int i = (int)tr.size - 1; i >= 0; --i)
            an += cost[i] * tr[i];
        cout << an << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
