**Hint 1:** Find out which number the $k$-th digit belongs to in the infinite sequence. Let this number be $n$. Instead of analyzing the sequence, we can calculate the sum of the sums of the digits of all integers from $0$ to $n - 1$ and add to this the sum of the required digits of the number $n$.

**Hint 2:** Mentally add leading zeros to all numbers from $0$ to $n - 1$ so that their lengths become the same. The sum of the sums of the digits will not change, but it will be more convenient to determine the formulas in this case.

First, determine the length $\ell$ of the number $n$ that the $k$-th digit falls into. We subtract out the total digits contributed by all numbers of shorter lengths (there are $9 \cdot 10^{\ell-1}$ numbers of length $\ell$, contributing $\ell \cdot 9 \cdot 10^{\ell-1}$ digits). Once we know $n$ and how many digits of it are included, we compute:

1. The sum of digits of the prefix of $n$ that is included.
2. The sum of digit sums of all numbers from the start of the $\ell$-digit range up to $n-1$.

For step 2, mentally pad all numbers with leading zeros to length $\ell$. For each digit position, the contribution can be computed analytically based on the digits of $n$.

```
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

int main {
    int t;
    cin >> t;

    while (t--) {
        long long k;
        cin >> k;
        long long cur = 9, len = 1;
        while (k - cur * len > 0) {
            k -= cur * len;
            cur *= 10;
            len++;
        }
        string s = to_string(cur / 9 + (k - 1) / len);
        long long ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < (k - 1) % len + 1; i++)
            ans += s[i] - '0';
        long long pr_s = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
            int curd = s[i] - '0';
            if (curd)
                ans += curd * (len - 1) * cur / 2 + curd * (2 * pr_s + curd - 1) / 2 * cur / 9;
            cur /= 10, len--;
            pr_s += curd;
        }
        cout << ans << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
