We need to find $y$ such that $(x \# y)$ is divisible by $x + y$. Note that $x \# y = x \cdot 10^d + y$ where $d$ is the number of digits in $y$. We need $(x + y) \mid (x \cdot 10^d + y)$. Since $x \cdot 10^d + y = x \cdot 10^d - x + x + y = x(10^d - 1) + (x+y)$, we need $(x+y) \mid x(10^d-1)$. Setting $y = 2x$, we get $x + y = 3x$, and $x \# 2x = x \cdot 10^d + 2x = x(10^d + 2)$. We need $3x \mid x(10^d+2)$, i.e., $3 \mid (10^d+2)$. Since $10 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$, we have $10^d + 2 \equiv 1 + 2 = 3 \equiv 0 \pmod{3}$. So $y = 2x$ always works.

