The key observation is that Alice goes first and Bob goes second. Alice wants to maximize $f(a)$, Bob wants to minimize it.

The expression $f(a) = \text{cost} + (a_1 - a_2 + a_3 - a_4 \cdots a_n)$ has alternating signs. A swap of $a_l$ and $a_r$ costs $r - l$ and changes the alternating sum.

The initial value without any swaps is $\text{init} = a_1 - a_2 + a_3 - \cdots$. Alice will make at most one swap (since Bob can end the game after Alice's move). Alice either ends immediately (getting $\text{init}$), or swaps some pair and Bob ends the game.

When Alice swaps indices $l$ and $r$ (1-indexed), the cost is $r - l$ and the change in the alternating sum depends on the parities of $l$ and $r$. The net change in $f(a)$ when swapping positions $i$ and $j$ (0-indexed) is: cost $= j - i$ (0-indexed), and the alternating sum changes by $\pm 2(a_i - a_j)$ depending on parities.

Alice picks the swap that maximizes the resulting $f(a)$. Using a sweep approach, we track the minimum of certain quantities to efficiently find the best swap.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
