Each pair of segments $[l_i, r_i]$ and $[l_j, r_j]$ chosen together produces a new segment $[x_k, y_k]$ with maximum length $r_j - l_i$ (choosing $x_k = l_i$, $y_k = r_j$ when $l_i \le r_j$). The original segments also contribute their own lengths.

The total contribution is: the sum of lengths of all original segments, plus $n/2$ pairs each contributing a new segment.

Each of the $n$ original segments contributes its length $(r_i - l_i)$ plus $1$ (from the $+1$ offset). For the paired segments, we want to maximize the sum of $y_k - x_k$ for each pair. We pair segments to maximize the sum of $(r_j - l_i)$ for each pair $(i, j)$. 

To maximize the sum of new segments, sort segments by $l_i + r_i$ and pair the $n/2$ segments with smallest $l_i + r_i$ as the "left" endpoints and the rest as "right" endpoints, taking their $r$ values. This way, for each pair, we maximize $r_j - l_i$.

For odd $n$, one segment is marked alone (contributing $0$ additional length from pairing), and we try all possibilities for which segment is left unpaired.

