Instead of directly transforming $s$ into $t$, is it enough to know how to transform any string into an intermediate string?

If you know how to turn any string into $0^n$ in at most $n$ operations, how can you use that to transform $s$ into $t$ in at most $2n$ operations?

If there is at least one position $p$ with $s_p = s_{p+1}$. Can you grow a block of equal bits starting from $[p, p+1]$?

How do you ensure such a position $p$ in strictly alternating string after exactly one operation?

When trying to grow the block to the right (or left), what happens if the next bit is equal to the block's boundary bit? What if it is different? Can you use flipping a palindromic block (all equal bits) to force the boundary to match the next bit?

The key idea: reduce any string to $0^n$ in at most $n$ operations, then use the reverse of those operations to go from $0^n$ to $t$. This gives at most $2n$ operations total.

To reduce a string $s$ to all zeros:
1. Find a position $p$ where $s_p = s_{p+1}$ (two consecutive equal characters). If no such position exists (the string is alternating), flip the first three characters (which form a palindrome of the form $010$ or $101$), creating such a position at index 2.
2. Maintain a block $[L, R]$ of all-equal characters. Extend right until $R = n-1$, then extend left until $L = 0$. When extending, if the next character differs from the block's value, first flip the current block (a palindrome of all equal characters) to match the next character, then extend.
3. Finally, if the resulting all-equal string is all ones, flip the entire string.

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

vector<array<int,2>> reduce(string &s){
    int n = s.size;
    vector<array<int,2>> op;

    int p = -1; // position p such that two consecutive characters are same
    for(int i = 0;i < n-1;i++){
        if(s[i] == s[i+1])p = i;
    }
    if(p == -1){ // string is alternating
        op.push_back({0,2}); // substring of first three character must be palindrome
        s[0] ^= 1;s[1] ^= 1;
        p = 2;
    }

    char value = s[p+1];
    int L = p,R = p+1; // We maintain [L,R] such that all position in it have the same value

    while(R+1 < n){
        if(s[R+1] != value)op.push_back({L,R});
        R++;value = s[R];
    }

    while(L){
        if(s[L-1] != value)op.push_back({L,R});
        L--;value = s[L];
    }

    if(value == '1')op.push_back({0,n-1}); // We want to make all zeroes

    return op;
}

void solve{
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    string s,t;
    cin >> s >> t;

    auto operation_s = reduce(s); // operations to reduce s to all zeroes
    auto operation_t = reduce(t); // operations to reduce t to all zeroes

    reverse(operation_t.begin,operation_t.end);

    int moves = operation_s.size + operation_t.size;
    cout << moves << "\n";
    for(auto &[x,y] : operation_s)cout << x+1 << " " << y+1 << "\n";
    for(auto &[x,y] : operation_t)cout << x+1 << " " << y+1 << "\n";

}

