When we apply the operation on range $[l, r]$, all elements in that range become $l + r$, contributing $(r - l + 1)(l + r)$ to the sum. We want to maximize the total sum. The gain from applying the operation on $[l, r]$ over keeping the original values is $(r - l + 1)(l + r) - \sum_{i=l}^{r} a_i$.

Using 1-indexed positions, we want to maximize $\sum_{i=l}^{r} (i \cdot 2 - a_i) - (r - l + 1) \cdot (... )$. Actually the gain is $\sum_{i=l}^{r} (l + r - a_i)$. For fixed $r$, this equals $(r+1)(r-l+1) + l(r-l+1) - \sum_{i=l}^{r} a_i$... Let $f(r) = (1+2+\dots+(r+1)) - \sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i$ using prefix structure.

The key insight: let $\text{cur}_i = (i+1)(i+2) - \text{prefix\_sum}(i)$ (using 0-indexed). We want to maximize $\text{cur}_r - \text{cur}_{l-1}$ over all $l \le r$, which is a maximum subarray-style computation. The answer is the original sum plus the best gain.

