We need to find the $k$-th remaining number after $x$ removal operations, where $x$ can be up to $10^{12}$, making the easy version's $O(x)$ simulation infeasible.

If $y = 1$, every element gets removed immediately, so output $-1$.

The key observation: after each operation, a number at position $p$ in the current sequence moves to position $p + \lfloor (p-1)/(y-1) \rfloor$ in the original sequence numbering (i.e., $k$ increases by approximately $\lfloor k/(y-1) \rfloor$ after each step... more precisely, the $k$-th remaining element in the new sequence corresponds to the $(k + \lfloor (k-1)/(y-1) \rfloor)$-th element before the operation).

The approach: we track what value $k$ becomes after each operation. After one operation on a sequence of length $n$, the $k$-th remaining element was at position $k + \lfloor (k-1)/(y-1) \rfloor$ before the operation. 

Since $k$ can grow slowly, we batch consecutive operations that produce the same increment $\lfloor (k-1)/(y-1) \rfloor = c$. Specifically, $c = \lfloor (k-1)/(y-1) \rfloor$ stays constant while $k < (c+1)(y-1) + 1$. We compute how many steps $cnt$ we can do with this same increment and advance $k$ by $cnt \cdot c$, adding $cnt$ to our step counter. If $k$ exceeds $10^{12}$, output $-1$.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

#define int long long

const int INF = 1'000'000'000'000LL;

void solve{
  int x, y, k;
  cin >> x >> y >> k;

  if (y == 1){
    cout << -1 << '\n';
    return;
  }

  for (int i = 0; i < x; ){
    int cur = (k - 1) / (y - 1);
    if (cur == 0){
      break;
    }
    int fk = (cur + 1) * (y - 1) + 1;
    int cnt = (fk - k + cur - 1) / cur;
    cnt = min(x - i, cnt);
    k += cnt * cur;
    if (k > INF){
      cout << -1 << '\n';
      return;
    }
    i += cnt;
  }

  cout << k << '\n';
}

