A beautiful array $b$ has $b_i \in a_i$ for each $i$. A perfect array $c$ must contain every beautiful array as a subsequence.

For $c$ to contain every beautiful $b$ as a subsequence, $c$ must contain, for each $i$, at least one occurrence of each element of $a_i$, in order. More precisely, $c$ must contain all elements of all arrays $a_i$ as subsequences in the right order.

The key insight: the perfect array $c$ must contain each value that appears in $a_i$ for each $i$. The elements within each $a_i$ can appear in $c$ in any order (since any permutation is a subsequence of $c$ that satisfies the requirement for array $i$). The array $c$ must be a supersequence of all beautiful arrays simultaneously.

For $c$ to be perfect, it must be a common supersequence of all beautiful arrays. The minimal such structure: $c$ must contain, for each group of arrays with the same set, at least $|\text{set}|$ elements (one of each). The order within each group can be arbitrary.

More precisely: classify arrays $a_i$ by their size $l_i \in \{1,2,3,4,5\}$. For an array of size $l$, all $l$ elements must appear in $c$ consecutively (as a block of $l$ distinct values). The number of arrays of each size determines how many such blocks are needed.

Let $\text{segments}[j]$ = number of arrays with $l_i = j$. The perfect array $c$ of length $n$ consists of blocks: for each group of size-$j$ arrays, we need $\text{segments}[j]$ blocks each of size $j$, plus we can fill remaining positions with arbitrary values from $\{1, \ldots, m\}$ that don't conflict.

The total number of "used" positions is $\sum_j j \cdot \text{segments}[j] = \sum_i l_i$. The remaining $n - \sum l_i$ positions can be filled with any of $m$ values, but we need to count arrangements carefully.

The generating function approach: for each size $j$ from 0 to 5, we have $\text{segments}[j]$ mandatory blocks of size $j$, each choosing $j$ distinct values from $m$ (with $m - j + 1$ choices for the first element... actually the block is an ordered sequence of $j$ distinct elements chosen from $m$). Wait — each $a_i$ of size $j$ contributes one block of $j$ specific elements (those in $a_i$) in any order ($j!$ orderings). The elements across different blocks can be freely arranged relative to each other and to free positions.

Actually, the values in the arrays are given, and the block content is fixed (the elements of $a_i$). The free positions can use any of $m$ values.

Let $\text{segments}[j]$ = number of arrays of size $j$. Then there are $\text{segments}[j]$ blocks of size $j$, each contributing $j!$ orderings. The number of ways to interleave all blocks and free positions in an array of length $n$:

The generating function for the number of ways to place $\text{segments}[j]$ indistinguishable blocks of size $j$ and any number of free elements from $m - j$ choices (elements not appearing in any size-$j$ block... but this gets complicated).

The solution counts: for $j$ from 0 to 5, define the generating function for placing $\text{segments}[j]$ blocks of size $j$ (each using $j$ positions) along with free elements from $m - j$ available values (those values that appear exactly 0 times as a required element in size-$j$ arrays... but values in size-$j$ blocks are "used"). The polynomial $\text{poly}[j]$ has coefficient of $x^k$ equal to $\binom{k + \text{segments}[j] - 1}{k} \cdot (m-j)^k$, representing choosing $k$ free elements from $m-j$ choices and interleaving with $\text{segments}[j]$ fixed blocks (stars and bars for the interleaving of $k$ free slots among $\text{segments}[j]$ blocks).

The final answer is: the product of all $\text{poly}[j]$, take the coefficient of $x^{n - \sum l_i}$, multiply by $\prod_i l_i!$ (for ordering within each block).

