Alice's winning strategy is related to bits. Alice's optimal number of moves depends only on the most significant bit and the number of set bits. The number $n$ is a power of two, which means if you subtract $1$, any subset of bits of $n-1$ will not exceed $n-1$.

For a number $a$ with most significant bit at position $\text{max\_bit}$ (0-indexed) and $\text{cnt\_bit}$ total set bits, Alice needs exactly $\text{max\_bit} + \text{cnt\_bit}$ moves to win. This is because:
- She needs $\text{max\_bit}$ halvings to reduce the leading bit to 0.
- She needs $\text{cnt\_bit}$ subtractions to clear all set bits.

The count of numbers from $1$ to $n = 2^d$ with a given $\text{max\_bit}$ and $\text{cnt\_bit}$ is $\binom{\text{max\_bit}}{\text{cnt\_bit} - 1}$ (choosing which of the lower bits are set). The number $2^d$ itself requires $d + 1$ moves.

We count all numbers where $\text{max\_bit} + \text{cnt\_bit} > k$.

