It is known that the first player loses if and only if the $xor$-sum of all numbers is $0$. Therefore the problem essentially asks to calculate the number of ways to arrange the cards in such a fashion that the $xor$-sum of the numbers on the upper sides of the cards is equal to zero.

Let $[formula]$ and $[formula]$. Suppose that the cards with indices $j_{1}, j_{2}, ..., j_{k}$ are faced with numbers of type $b$ and all the others with numbers of type $a$. Then the $xor$-sum of this arrangement is equal to $[formula]$, that is, $[formula]$. Hence we want to find the number of subsets $c_{i}$ with $xor$-sum of $S$.

Note that we can replace $c_{1}$ with $[formula]$, as applying $c_{1}$ is the same as applying $[formula]$. Thus we can freely replace ${c_{1}, c_{2}}$ with $[formula]$ and $c_{2}$ with $[formula]$. This means that we can apply the following procedure to simplify the set of $c_{i}$:

• Pick $c_{f}$ with the most significant bit set to one
• Replace each $c_{i}$ with the bit in that position set to one to $[formula]$
• Remove $c_{f}$ from the set
• Repeat steps $1$-$5$ with the remaining set
• Add $c_{f}$ back to the set

After this procedure we get a set that contains $k$ zeros and $n - k$ numbers with the property that the positions of the most significant bit set to one strictly decrease. How do we check now whether it is possible to obtain a subset with $xor$-sum $S$? As we have at most one number with a one in the most significant bit, then it tells us whether we should include that number in the subset or not. Similarly we apply the same argument for all other bits. If we don't obtain a subset with the $xor$-sum equal to $S$, then there is no such subset at all. If we do get a subset with $xor$-sum $S$, then the total number of such subsets is equal to $2^{k}$, as for each of the $n - k$ non-zero numbers we already know whether it must be include in such a subset or not, but any subset of $k$ zeros doesn't change the $xor$-sum. In this case the probability of the second player winning the game is equal to $[formula]$, so the first player wins with probability $[formula]$.

